{
  "title": "Stage 4: Sessions",
  "summary": "Write your own iterator with yield to cut sessions at time gaps, and pair neighbours with Zip.",
  "minutes": 18,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Briefing: sessions and your own iterator",
      "body": [
        "A **session** is a run of requests from one IP with no long silence in between. Here: a gap of more than 30 minutes starts a new session.",
        "",
        "No built-in LINQ method cuts a sequence where the gap between neighbours is too big, so you write one. A method that uses `yield return` is an **iterator**: it hands items out one at a time, and its code only runs as the caller asks for the next item.",
        "",
        "```csharp",
        "IEnumerable<List<Entry>> Sessions(IEnumerable<Entry> ordered, TimeSpan gap)",
        "{",
        "    var current = new List<Entry>();",
        "    foreach (var e in ordered)",
        "    {",
        "        if (current.Count > 0 && e.Time - current[^1].Time > gap)",
        "        {",
        "            yield return current;          // hand out the finished session",
        "            current = new List<Entry>();",
        "        }",
        "        current.Add(e);",
        "    }",
        "    if (current.Count > 0)",
        "        yield return current;              // the last one",
        "}",
        "```",
        "",
        "Because it returns `IEnumerable`, it plugs straight into LINQ: `Sessions(...).Count()`, `.Select(s => s.Count)`, and so on."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "An iterator runs only as items are asked for. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "IEnumerable<int> Numbers()",
        "{",
        "    Console.WriteLine(\"start\");",
        "    yield return 1;",
        "    Console.WriteLine(\"between\");",
        "    yield return 2;",
        "    Console.WriteLine(\"end\");",
        "}",
        "",
        "var first = Numbers().First();",
        "Console.WriteLine($\"got {first}\");"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "start",
        "got 1"
      ],
      "explain": "`First()` asks for one item. The iterator runs up to the first `yield return` and is then abandoned, so `between` and `end` never print."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "Pairing each item with the next one using `Zip`. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var times = new[] { 0, 5, 50, 52 };",
        "",
        "var gaps = times.Zip(times.Skip(1), (earlier, later) => later - earlier);",
        "",
        "Console.WriteLine(string.Join(\" \", gaps));",
        "Console.WriteLine(gaps.Max());"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "5 45 2",
        "45"
      ],
      "explain": "`times.Skip(1)` is the same list shifted by one, so `Zip` pairs each item with its neighbour: (0,5), (5,50), (50,52). `Zip` stops when the shorter sequence ends."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "`Chunk` cuts into fixed-size pieces, unlike your gap-based `Sessions`. What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "var ids = new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 };",
        "",
        "foreach (var batch in ids.Chunk(3))",
        "    Console.WriteLine(string.Join(\",\", batch));"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "1,2,3",
        "4,5,6",
        "7"
      ],
      "explain": "`Chunk(3)` yields arrays of three, and the last chunk holds whatever is left. It is the right tool for batching, but sessions need cuts based on the data, which is why you write your own iterator."
    },
